===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|originally|India}} Originally, a [[three-legged]] [[decorative#Adjective|decorative]] [[stand#Noun|stand]] or [[table#Noun|table]]; now, especially, one with [[recess#Noun|recesses]] for [[hold#Verb|holding]] [[tea caddy|tea caddies]] and/or a [[tea service]]. {{defdate|from 1820s}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Nancy E. Davis|chapter=Afong Moy Presents Chinese Objects for the Home|title=The Chinese Lady: {{w|Afong Moy}} in Early America|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher={{w|Oxford University Press}}|year=2019|section=part II (The Show)|pages=107–108|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=IdGaDwAAQBAJ&pg=PA107|isbn=978-0-19-064523-6|passage=The '''teapoy''', derived from the Hindi/Persian phrase denoting a three-footed table, supported a tea set or tea-related objects. Not all '''teapoys''' were tripod-like tables or sold singly. The Carneses imported lacquer '''teapoys''' in sets. These sets could be easily stacked in a corner of the drawing room and brought out at teatime to hold a teacup, a set, or a caddy. The Carneses purchased lacquered '''teapoys''' sets for four dollars in China and probably sold them for twice that amount in America.}}

